# Joining an enemy of a faction you're in (DESIGN.md §9.2): the faction you're leaving must
# let you go (its deserters table), and the one you're joining must take you (its defectors
# table). Rules are tried top to bottom; the first whose conditions all hold decides.
load crates/cli/tests/fixtures/worlds/defectors
# Vex has reformed: 35.09 from the Watch, within its 40, and 95.52 from the Guild, past its
# 60. The Guild releases him (rule 2) and the Watch takes him (rule 2), at a cost of 10.
can-join vex city_watch --explain
join vex city_watch
assert show character vex == vex — Vex — law 35.00, good 10.00 — Lawful Neutral — member of city_watch (recruit) since tick 0
assert standing vex city_watch == -10.00
# Kestrel is just as reformed, but a shadow: officers don't walk away.
assert can-join kestrel city_watch == no: kestrel belongs to The Lantern Guild, in conflict with The City Watch (-80.00): refused by The Lantern Guild's deserters rule 1, "Officers don't walk away."
# The Watch already trusts Nell, so its first rule takes her, at no cost.
join nell city_watch
# The Circle's own deserters table replaces the world's: no one leaves. The Temple's own
# defectors table would take Ash, naming one of its ranks: below "ordained" means rung 1.
can-join brother_ash temple --explain
journal

# In Riverhold, a thief who joined the Guild gets no further with the Watch: no standing
# with it, and nearer the Guild (40.01) than the Watch (90.35), so its last rule refuses.
load content/sample
act player steal --scale 4
join player lantern_guild
assert can-join player city_watch == no: 90.35 from The City Watch, tolerance is 40.00; player belongs to The Lantern Guild, in conflict with The City Watch (-80.00): refused by The City Watch's defectors rule 3, "You serve our enemies."
